  "response": "# Verdict: REVISE\n\n## Summary\n\nThe component demonstrates good baseline awareness of accessibility primitives (native `<form>`, `<label htmlFor>`, explicit `aria-invalid`, `aria-describedby` error linkages, and visible focus rings). However, critical and major design flaws undermine the user experience across assistive technology, keyboard, and cognitive perspectives. \n\nKey failures include:\n1. **Focus trapping / loss on disabled controls**: Disabling the \"Next\" button immediately upon errors while a user is on it or navigating traps or drops focus, and prevents users from discovering *why* progress is blocked.\n2. **Step hiding via visually-hidden clip instead of `display: none` / `inert`**: The inactive panel elements remain focusable in the keyboard Tab order because `aria-hidden=\"true\"` does not remove elements from keyboard sequence.\n3. **No focus management across step transitions**: Moving forward or backward leaves keyboard/screen-reader focus stranded on the trigger button without navigating to the new step's heading or first input.\n4. **Step indicator semantics**: Using `<span>` without `aria-current=\"step\"` or list semantics fails standard wizard guidance.\n5. **Missing field autocomplete**: Personal data inputs (`name`, `email`, `street-address`, `postal-code`) lack `autocomplete` attributes.\n\n---\n\n## Pre-commitment Predictions\n\nBefore auditing the code:\n- **Prediction 1**: Step indicator will lack programmatic identification of the active step (`aria-current=\"step\"`) and list semantics (`<ol>`).\n- **Prediction 2**: Hidden steps will use CSS hiding or `aria-hidden=\"true\"` without disabling or removing hidden interactive inputs from the Tab order, causing ghost tab stops.\n- **Prediction 3**: Focus will not be managed across step transitions (users remain on the \"Next\" or \"Back\" button after step changes).\n- **Prediction 4**: Disabled \"Next\" button will cause keyboard focus loss or obscure validation feedback for keyboard/screen reader users.\n- **Prediction 5**: Live region announcements will be erratic or silent when error items are removed dynamically.\n\n---\n\n## Findings\n\n### Critical Findings (Blocks access)\n\n1. **Hidden Step Inputs Remain in Keyboard Tab Order (`aria-hidden` without `inert` or `display: none`)**\n   - **Location**: `MultiStepForm.jsx:54-55`, `MultiStepForm.jsx:70-71`, and CSS `.step-hidden`\n   - **Impacted Users**: Keyboard-only users, low vision users using keyboard navigation.\n   - **WCAG / APG Citation**: WCAG 2.1.1 (Keyboard), WCAG 2.4.3 (Focus Order).\n   - **Why this matters**: Inactive panels receive `.step-hidden`, which is implemented as a 1px clipped off-screen element (`clip: rect(0, 0, 0, 0)`), with `aria-hidden=\"true\"`. **`aria-hidden=\"true\"` does NOT remove elements from the browser keyboard Tab sequence**. When on Step 0, a keyboard user pressing Tab after the \"Next\" button will tab directly into the invisible inputs of Step 1 (`#street` and `#zip`), causing focus to vanish off-screen. Furthermore, screen reader accessibility trees will produce conflicting behavior between focus events and `aria-hidden`.\n   - **Confidence**: HIGH\n   - **Fix**: Use conditional rendering `{step === 0 && (...)}` or apply `display: none` / HTML `inert` attribute / HTML `hidden` attribute to inactive steps:\n     ```jsx\n     <div className=\"step-panel\" hidden={step !== 0}>\n     ```\n     Or in React:\n     ```jsx\n     {step === 0 && (\n       <div className=\"step-panel\">...</div>\n     )}\n     ```\n\n2. **Next Button Hard-Disabled Upon Error (`disabled={hasErrors}`) Traps Focus & Blocks Discoverability**\n   - **Location**: `MultiStepForm.jsx:99`\n   - **Impacted Users**: Keyboard-only users, screen reader users.\n   - **WCAG / APG Citation**: WCAG 2.1.1 (Keyboard), WCAG 4.1.2 (Name, Role, Value), WAI-ARIA APG Form Validation Practices.\n   - **Why this matters**: When the user focuses the \"Next\" button and presses Enter/Space, `validate()` sets errors, which immediately triggers `hasErrors = true` and applies `disabled={hasErrors}` to the button. In standard browsers, disabling an actively focused button drops focus to `<body>`, resetting keyboard position to the top of the window. The user loses their place entirely and must tab through the whole page again. Additionally, when disabled, screen readers cannot tab to the button to discover that proceeding is blocked, nor can the button trigger validation messages again.\n   - **Confidence**: HIGH\n   - **Fix**: Never hard-disable the button in response to validation errors. Keep the button operable (`disabled={false}`), re-run validation on click, and focus the first invalid input or the error summary when validation fails:\n     ```jsx\n     <button type=\"button\" className=\"btn-next\" onClick={next}>\n       Next\n     </button>\n     ```\n\n---\n\n### Major Findings (Significantly degrades experience)\n\n1. **Lack of Focus Management on Step Progression and Reversal**\n   - **Location**: `MultiStepForm.jsx:31-32` (`next` and `back` functions)\n   - **Impacted Users**: Screen reader users, keyboard-only users, cognitive disability users.\n   - **WCAG / APG Citation**: WCAG 2.4.3 (Focus Order), WCAG 3.2.1 (On Focus).\n   - **Why this matters**: When advancing from Step 0 to Step 1, focus stays on the \"Next\" button. Screen reader users receive no notification that the view has changed to \"Address\" unless they manually navigate backwards into the DOM. Similarly, clicking \"Back\" leaves focus on the \"Back\" button or shifts unpredictably.\n   - **Confidence**: HIGH\n   - **Fix**: Direct focus to the new step's `<h2>` heading (with `tabIndex={-1}`) or to the first field of the new step whenever `step` changes:\n     ```jsx\n     const stepHeadingRef = useRef(null);\n     useEffect(() => {\n       stepHeadingRef.current?.focus();\n     }, [step]);\n     ```\n\n2. **Step Indicator Missing List Semantics and Programmatic Current State**\n   - **Location**: `MultiStepForm.jsx:38-46`\n   - **Impacted Users**: Screen reader users.\n   - **WCAG / APG Citation**: WCAG 1.3.1 (Info and Relationships), WCAG 4.1.2 (Name, Role, Value).\n   - **Why this matters**: The progress indicator is a group of unstructured `<span>` elements. Screen reader users are told it is a \"Form progress\" group, but not how many items exist in total or that they represent an ordered sequence of steps. Active state is styled visually via `.active` without `aria-current=\"step\"`.\n   - **Confidence**: HIGH\n   - **Fix**: Structure the indicator as an `<ol>` navigation/progress landmark, and add `aria-current=\"step\"` to the active step:\n     ```jsx\n     <nav aria-label=\"Form progress\">\n       <ol className=\"step-indicator\">\n         {STEPS.map((label, i) => (\n           <li key={label} className={`step-dot ${i === step ? 'active' : ''}`}>\n             <span {...(i === step ? { 'aria-current': 'step' } : {})}>\n               {label} ({i + 1} of {STEPS.length})\n             </span>\n           </li>\n         ))}\n       </ol>\n     </nav>\n     ```\n\n3. **Missing `autocomplete` Attributes on Standard Personal Data Fields**\n   - **Location**: `MultiStepForm.jsx:58`, `MultiStepForm.jsx:65`, `MultiStepForm.jsx:74`, `MultiStepForm.jsx:80`\n   - **Impacted Users**: Cognitive disabilities, motor disabilities.\n   - **WCAG / APG Citation**: WCAG 1.3.5 (Identify Input Purpose - Level AA), WCAG 3.3.7 (Redundant Entry).\n   - **Why this matters**: WCAG 2.1/2.2 AA requires input fields collecting user info to convey their purpose programmatically to support browser autofill and assistive software.\n   - **Confidence**: HIGH\n   - **Fix**: Add appropriate autofill tokens:\n     - `name`: `autoComplete=\"name\"`\n     - `email`: `autoComplete=\"email\"`\n     - `street`: `autoComplete=\"street-address\"`\n     - `zip`: `autoComplete=\"postal-code\"`\n\n4. **Error Summary List Lacks In-Page Anchor Navigation to Fields**\n   - **Location**: `MultiStepForm.jsx:47-53`\n   - **Impacted Users**: Screen reader users, keyboard users, cognitive accessibility users.\n   - **WCAG / APG Citation**: WCAG 3.3.1 (Error Identification), WCAG 3.3.3 (Error Suggestion), WAI-ARIA APG Error Summary Pattern.\n   - **Why this matters**: When multiple errors appear in the summary, keyboard and screen reader users cannot quickly jump directly to the invalid input from the error list item. Standard error summaries should render links (e.g., `<a href=\"#name\">Full name is required.</a>`) that focus the invalid control on click.\n   - **Confidence**: HIGH\n   - **Fix**: Make error summary entries jump links to corresponding inputs:\n     ```jsx\n     <ul className=\"error-list\">\n       {Object.entries(errors).map(([k, msg]) => (\n         <li key={k}>\n           <a href={`#${k}`}>{msg}</a>\n         </li>\n       ))}\n     </ul>\n     ```\n\n---\n\n### Minor Findings (Friction but workaround exists)\n\n1. **Step Indicator Color Contrast for Inactive and Active Steps**\n   - **Location**: CSS `.step-dot` (`#94a3b8` on `#f8fafc`) and `.step-dot.active` (`#1e40af` on `#dbeafe`)\n   - **Impacted Users**: Low vision users.\n   - **WCAG Citation**: WCAG 1.4.3 (Contrast - Minimum).\n   - **Why this matters**: `#94a3b8` (slate-400) on `#f8fafc` has a contrast ratio of ~2.5:1, failing the 4.5:1 requirement for normal text.\n   - **Fix**: Darken inactive step text to `#64748b` or `#475569` (meeting at least 4.5:1).\n\n2. **Unused `liveRef` Ref Object**\n   - **Location**: `MultiStepForm.jsx:9`, `MultiStepForm.jsx:47`\n   - **Impacted Users**: Maintainers / implementation cleanliness.\n   - **Why this matters**: `liveRef` is initialized and attached to `div.error-region` but never used for programmatic focus or announcements.\n   - **Fix**: Either focus `liveRef.current` when errors occur or remove the unused ref.\n\n---\n\n## What's Missing\n\n1. **Focus Shift to First Invalid Input or Error Summary**: When validation fails, focus remains wherever it was (or drops if Next is disabled). The first invalid input should be focused so the user can immediately correct it.\n2. **Review Step Edit Links**: In Step 2 (Confirm), the user sees read-only representations of their data (`<dl>`), but there are no direct \"Edit\" buttons to return directly to Step 0 or Step 1 without repeatedly clicking the \"Back\" button.\n3. **Form Submission State**: Step 2 has `<button type=\"submit\">Submit</button>`, but `form onSubmit` only calls `e.preventDefault()`. There is no loading indication, submission status announcement, or success state communicated to assistive technology.\n\n---\n\n## Multi-Perspective Summary\n\n| Perspective | Alarm Level | Trigger Signal |\n|-------------|-------------|----------------|\n| **Screen reader** | **HIGH** | Inactive steps exposed or confusing; no focus transfer across steps; active step lacks `aria-current=\"step\"`. |\n| **Keyboard-only** | **HIGH** | Hidden inputs in `.step-hidden` remain focusable via Tab; `disabled` Next button causes focus drop to `<body>`. |\n| **Low vision** | **MEDIUM** | Inactive step indicator text contrast (`#94a3b8`) fails 4.5:1 ratio. |\n| **Cognitive** | **MEDIUM** | Missing `autocomplete` attributes; missing jump links from error summary to inputs. |\n| **Vestibular & motion** | **LOW** | No animations or auto-playing movement present. |\n| **Auditory access** | **LOW** | No audio or video media present. |\n| **Environmental contrast** | **MEDIUM** | Slate-400 text fails contrast against light slate background. |\n\n- **Screen Reader User**: Navigating forward does not announce the new step context or move virtual cursor to the new inputs. When errors occur, if Next is disabled, navigation is disorienting.\n- **Keyboard-only User**: Tabbing past Next tabs into invisible clipped inputs in the inactive panel. Disabling Next button drops focus.\n- **Low Vision User**: Step numbers in the progress indicator are hard to read due to low contrast.\n- **Cognitive Accessibility**: Inability to use autofill increases friction and error rates for address and contact details.\n\n---\n\n## Verdict Justification\n\n**Verdict: REVISE**\n\nWhile the author correctly applied element-level attributes like `aria-invalid`, `aria-describedby`, and `<label htmlFor>`, the component exhibits major structural and interactional accessibility gaps:\n1. `aria-hidden` does not prevent keyboard focus into off-screen panels (`clip` without `display: none` or `inert`), violating WCAG 2.1.1 and 2.4.3.\n2. Hard-disabling the Next button upon validation error causes loss of keyboard focus to `<body>`.\n3. Inactive steps and progress states lack standard WAI-ARIA multi-step wizard navigation semantics.\n\nTo upgrade this component to **ACCEPT**, the following changes must be implemented:\n- Ensure inactive steps are truly inert or conditionally rendered/unmounted (`hidden`, `inert`, or `{step === i && ...}`).\n- Keep the \"Next\" button enabled so focus is maintained, and move focus to the first invalid input or error summary upon validation failure.\n- Implement programmatic focus shift to the step heading on step change.\n- Add `autoComplete` attributes to standard personal data inputs.\n- Add `<ol>` and `aria-current=\"step\"` to the step indicator.\n",
